00:02:20.654 --> 00:02:25.964
Use the chat to connect with
other attendees Hi, everyone.

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:30.954
My name is Mia Salazar, and my talk is
called Accessibility and Neurodivergence:

00:02:31.018 --> 00:02:32.887
Not Everyone Navigates the Same Way.

00:02:33.488 --> 00:02:35.935
To begin with, I'm going
to introduce myself.

00:02:36.274 --> 00:02:37.351
I'm Mia Salazar.

00:02:37.405 --> 00:02:40.458
I have been a front-end developer
for eight years now, and I'm

00:02:40.466 --> 00:02:42.094
specializing in accessibility.

00:02:42.649 --> 00:02:45.236
Also, I'm one of the
organizers of the a11yConf.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:49.418
The a11yConf is the conference
in Spain about accessibility.

00:02:49.757 --> 00:02:51.992
On the other hand, I'm a
mentor at Step Forward.

00:02:52.086 --> 00:02:57.123
In Step Forward, we mentor women that
work in tech or that want to work in tech.

00:02:57.838 --> 00:03:01.352
And now, what is neurodivergent
and what is neurotypical?

00:03:01.906 --> 00:03:05.772
There are two types of brains,
neurodivergent brains and neurotypical

00:03:05.777 --> 00:03:10.001
brains, and each type process
the stimuli and the information

00:03:10.005 --> 00:03:11.767
and the data in a different way.

00:03:12.216 --> 00:03:14.481
Neither type of them is
better than the other.

00:03:14.795 --> 00:03:19.368
In fact, there are some evo- evolutionary
studies that suggest that both brains

00:03:19.368 --> 00:03:23.933
make sense because while neurodivergent
brains have some strengths and

00:03:23.933 --> 00:03:28.772
weakness, neurotypical brain has a
different set of strengths and weakness.

00:03:28.892 --> 00:03:33.635
So that way, having both brains makes
sense because they complement each other.

00:03:33.763 --> 00:03:36.051
And now we are going to
talk about statistics.

00:03:36.233 --> 00:03:40.719
Between 10 and 20% of the population
are neurodivergent people.

00:03:40.814 --> 00:03:48.738
And here is the most impressive statistic:
50%, in fact, more than 50% of people that

00:03:48.751 --> 00:03:51.581
work in tech are neurodivergent people.

00:03:51.671 --> 00:03:56.072
So now we are going to talk about
some types of neurodivergences.

00:03:56.541 --> 00:04:00.917
First, ASD, autism spectrum disorder.

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:05.779
About 3.2% of children
are autistic children.

00:04:06.613 --> 00:04:10.952
Autistic people socially interact
in a different way, they communicate

00:04:10.972 --> 00:04:14.799
in another way, and they respond to
sensory stimulation in a different way.

00:04:15.564 --> 00:04:21.985
Now, ADHD, attention deficit
hyperactivity dis- disorder.

00:04:22.685 --> 00:04:27.462
About five, 5.9% of people have ADHD.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:29.289
Of, of children, sorry.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:32.113
About 5.9% of children.

00:04:32.764 --> 00:04:36.736
Uh, people with ADHD have their
executive functions impacted.

00:04:37.326 --> 00:04:40.754
They have problems when organizing
tasks, uh, starting and finishing

00:04:40.789 --> 00:04:44.514
tasks, concentrating, and
controlling their own impulses.

00:04:45.864 --> 00:04:46.881
Now, dyslexia.

00:04:47.208 --> 00:04:49.850
20% of youth have dyslexia.

00:04:50.436 --> 00:04:52.212
Dyslexia is a learning disorder.

00:04:52.307 --> 00:04:55.995
It affects reading and writing,
and it can affect learning

00:04:56.013 --> 00:04:57.719
how to read and how to write.

00:04:59.236 --> 00:04:59.901
Dyspraxia.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.641
6% of children has dyspraxia.

00:05:03.247 --> 00:05:08.513
And right now you might be wondering why
I always giving you data about children.

00:05:08.746 --> 00:05:13.526
Well, that's why neurodivergences
are understudied in adults.

00:05:13.688 --> 00:05:18.169
In fact, it's pretty difficult to
give a diagnose to an adult when we

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:19.895
are talking about neurodivergences.

00:05:20.611 --> 00:05:26.901
So we have to push the society to keep
researching on neurodivergences on adults.

00:05:27.337 --> 00:05:31.835
But going back to dyspraxia,
dyspraxia is a psychomotor disorder.

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:37.842
It affect movements and the coordination
of those movements Dyscalculia.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:42.034
Between 3 and 7% of
population have dyscalculia.

00:05:42.173 --> 00:05:44.429
Here, I'm giving you data about adults.

00:05:45.431 --> 00:05:47.337
Dyscalculia is a learning disorder.

00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:49.251
It affects numerical processing.

00:05:49.813 --> 00:05:55.090
People with dyscalculia have problems
when doing subtraction, addition,

00:05:55.101 --> 00:06:00.371
multiplication, any mathematical
operation or any numerical processing.

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:03.510
And lastly, Tourette syndrome.

00:06:03.825 --> 00:06:07.525
0.6% of children has Tourette syndrome.

00:06:08.613 --> 00:06:12.693
Tourette syndrome provokes repetitive
and involuntary movements and sounds.

00:06:12.751 --> 00:06:14.460
They are kind of tics.

00:06:16.013 --> 00:06:17.505
And now I want to pause.

00:06:17.888 --> 00:06:20.938
These are the most common
types of neurodivergences.

00:06:21.027 --> 00:06:26.246
However, I would like to talk
about anxiety and depression, too.

00:06:26.936 --> 00:06:31.842
There is a lot of debate about this
topic, however, anxiety and depression

00:06:31.844 --> 00:06:33.950
are not considered neurodivergences.

00:06:34.522 --> 00:06:38.803
Nevertheless, I would like to talk
about it because a brain that suffers

00:06:38.817 --> 00:06:43.567
from anxiety and depression can
behave in certain scenarios as a

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:46.145
brain that, uh, has a neurodivergence.

00:06:47.118 --> 00:06:51.583
Besides, a brain that, uh, suffers
from anxiety and from depression can

00:06:51.589 --> 00:06:54.998
benefits from the measures we are
going to do for neurodivergent people.

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.181
So anxiety.

00:06:57.729 --> 00:07:01.513
5.4% of people have anxiety.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:05.109
Anxiety provokes excessive
worries, intrusive thoughts,

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:08.751
and it can lead to physical
tension, sweating or palpitations.

00:07:08.989 --> 00:07:09.669
Depression.

00:07:10.101 --> 00:07:13.485
5.7% of people have depression.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:19.832
Depression provokes a deep sadness that
can affect both mood and energy, and

00:07:19.832 --> 00:07:24.219
some of the studies suggest that it
can slow down or reduce brain activity.

00:07:24.486 --> 00:07:26.907
And now, I would like
to mention comorbidity.

00:07:27.373 --> 00:07:28.460
What is comorbidity?

00:07:29.418 --> 00:07:33.248
Comorbidity is the preexistence
of multiple disease, or

00:07:33.279 --> 00:07:37.010
in this case, of multiple
neurodivergences at the same time.

00:07:37.734 --> 00:07:41.317
Comorbidity is pretty common
among neurodivergent people.

00:07:41.433 --> 00:07:47.054
In fact, between 50 and 70% of
autistic people also have ADHD.

00:07:47.852 --> 00:07:52.344
Besides, anxiety and depression are
pretty common among neurodivergent

00:07:52.366 --> 00:07:58.105
people, so we just cannot simply create
a product that is accessible for,

00:07:58.287 --> 00:08:02.712
for example, a, an autistic person
because maybe that person also have

00:08:02.746 --> 00:08:05.887
ADHD, dyspraxia, anxiety or depression.

00:08:06.278 --> 00:08:09.333
We have to take into account
that neurodivergences should

00:08:09.345 --> 00:08:10.640
be treated as a whole.

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:13.051
And now, I want to
introduce you to Manuela.

00:08:13.615 --> 00:08:15.963
Manuela is the protagonist of our history.

00:08:16.154 --> 00:08:20.213
Manuela is a neurodivergent person
that has anxiety and depression,

00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:22.304
and she lives in Sardinia, Italy.

00:08:22.591 --> 00:08:26.192
Manuela is in a long-distance
relationship with her girlfriend, Ana.

00:08:26.878 --> 00:08:31.260
She haven't met yet, but she wants
to visit her, so she's, she is

00:08:31.265 --> 00:08:35.326
going to go to her favorite airline
w- website to buy the tickets.

00:08:36.316 --> 00:08:40.957
However, when she arrives to that
airline website, she cannot buy those

00:08:40.981 --> 00:08:44.798
tickets because this website is not
accessible for neurodivergent people.

00:08:45.484 --> 00:08:47.425
However, don't panic.

00:08:48.115 --> 00:08:53.500
By a twist of fate, Ana worked on that
airline company, and she talks to her

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:58.094
boss, and they both agree that Ana and
Manuela work together to improve the

00:08:58.094 --> 00:09:02.665
accessibility of this website to make
it accessible for neurodivergent people.

00:09:02.792 --> 00:09:06.969
And the first thing Manuela tells
her is that the color palette

00:09:06.997 --> 00:09:09.085
itself is not accessible at all.

00:09:09.718 --> 00:09:13.541
Firstly, because they are using a
blue and a yellow that are too bright.

00:09:14.206 --> 00:09:18.895
Too bright colors can cause anxiety and
can be overwhelming for autistic people.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.887
Besides, they are using absolute
white and absolute black.

00:09:23.401 --> 00:09:28.308
Absolute white and absolute bla-
black, when they are together, when

00:09:28.309 --> 00:09:31.674
we have, for example, a background
black, absolute white, and the text

00:09:31.675 --> 00:09:35.912
on, um, absolute white, is difficult
to process for dyslexic people.

00:09:36.059 --> 00:09:42.336
So they suggest to change the whole color
palette, avoiding using, uh, bright colors

00:09:42.348 --> 00:09:47.795
such as the, the blue, the, the bright
blue and the bright yellow, and avoiding

00:09:47.796 --> 00:09:49.806
using absolute white and absolute black.

00:09:50.723 --> 00:09:55.814
And now is the time for the logotype
itself, because Manuela points out that

00:09:55.814 --> 00:10:00.328
the logotype itself is not accessible
because they are using capital letters.

00:10:01.201 --> 00:10:05.690
Capital letters are not accessible
because people with anxiety might

00:10:05.694 --> 00:10:07.433
think that someone is yelling at them.

00:10:08.177 --> 00:10:13.065
And as all the letters are the same
height, it can be difficult for a dyslexic

00:10:13.069 --> 00:10:17.545
people, dyslexic per- person to determine
which is the shape of each letter.

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:21.388
So they decide to erase
the capital letters.

00:10:21.903 --> 00:10:28.229
But now Manuela suggests that they avoid
using cursive letters, and that is slanted

00:10:28.231 --> 00:10:33.286
letters, because slanted letters are
hard to process for dyslexic people and

00:10:33.287 --> 00:10:35.484
can be overwhelming for autistic people.

00:10:35.996 --> 00:10:38.856
So again, they decide to make this change.

00:10:39.263 --> 00:10:45.614
But now Manuela is telling Ana that
the typography itself is not accessible

00:10:45.656 --> 00:10:47.809
because this typography is too decorative.

00:10:48.538 --> 00:10:52.849
Too decorative typographies, it depends
on the typography, can be overwhelming

00:10:52.856 --> 00:10:57.153
for autistic people, can cause anxiety,
and are going to be really hard to

00:10:57.153 --> 00:11:03.680
process for dyslexic peop- So they
decide to change the whole typography.

00:11:04.359 --> 00:11:08.473
However, the typography that
Ana has suggested to use to

00:11:08.486 --> 00:11:10.933
Manuela is not accessible.

00:11:11.622 --> 00:11:12.040
Why?

00:11:12.085 --> 00:11:14.185
Because this typography has serif.

00:11:14.466 --> 00:11:18.570
For those who doesn't know what
serif is, are the tails that can

00:11:18.578 --> 00:11:20.820
be seen at the end of each letter.

00:11:21.338 --> 00:11:25.563
These tails can make it difficult
to process for dyslexic people.

00:11:25.622 --> 00:11:29.154
It depends on the typography,
of course, and it can be

00:11:29.154 --> 00:11:30.704
overwhelming for autistic people.

00:11:30.915 --> 00:11:35.617
So at the end, Ana and Manuela work
together, and they decide to change

00:11:35.692 --> 00:11:39.957
all the typographies of this website,
and they choose two typographies.

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:45.535
The first typography is going to be used
for the logo type and for the headlines.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:50.157
The first typography is a little bit
more decorative, but as it's only

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:54.416
going to be used on the logo types
and the headlines and both of them

00:11:54.505 --> 00:11:59.685
are going to be bigger, it's not going
to be as hard to process as, uh, the

00:11:59.689 --> 00:12:01.483
typography we, we have seen before.

00:12:02.103 --> 00:12:06.034
And the second typography is going
to be used on all the rest of the

00:12:06.034 --> 00:12:08.040
text, on the paragraphs itself.

00:12:08.417 --> 00:12:12.751
And they have chosen a really clear
and simple typography without serif.

00:12:13.012 --> 00:12:16.054
So now we can see that
it looks so much better.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:18.103
We have changed the color, the typography.

00:12:18.117 --> 00:12:19.747
It looks pretty nice.

00:12:20.192 --> 00:12:24.752
However, there is still a long way
to make this website accessible

00:12:25.113 --> 00:12:28.609
because now Manuela wants to
talk about the line height.

00:12:28.865 --> 00:12:32.109
That is the spaces between each lines.

00:12:32.635 --> 00:12:35.953
Right now, the lines are really
too close to each other, and this

00:12:35.959 --> 00:12:40.478
situation can cause anxiety, can be
overwhelming for autistic people.

00:12:40.944 --> 00:12:44.935
And people with dyslexia are going to
have a hard time reading these lines

00:12:44.971 --> 00:12:49.029
because they might read the incorrect
line because they might mix together.

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:54.798
So they decide to add more
spaces between each line This

00:12:54.802 --> 00:12:56.348
way, this looks so much better.

00:12:57.125 --> 00:13:01.812
And now Manuela wants to point
out that the alignment they

00:13:01.812 --> 00:13:03.347
are using is not accessible.

00:13:04.106 --> 00:13:06.771
Right now they are using
justified alignment.

00:13:07.181 --> 00:13:11.951
Justified alignment is qua- is where
all lines end at the same point.

00:13:12.668 --> 00:13:17.894
However, to make all those lines end
at the same point, we create wider

00:13:17.896 --> 00:13:22.255
spaces between words, and that creates
what is called the river effect.

00:13:22.361 --> 00:13:27.175
The river effect happens when we join
together these wider spaces between

00:13:27.197 --> 00:13:32.125
words with the wider spaces between
word of the next li- of the next line,

00:13:32.126 --> 00:13:34.842
creating a river that flows to the bottom.

00:13:35.571 --> 00:13:40.601
That river effect can be distracting for
people with ADHD, can be overwhelming

00:13:40.604 --> 00:13:44.777
for autistic people, and is really
hard to process for dyslexic people.

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:49.943
So Manuela tells her that they
shouldn't change, they should change it.

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:53.357
And Ana proposed to use
the center alignment.

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:59.723
However, centered alignment is also
inaccessible because each line starts

00:13:59.781 --> 00:14:04.442
at a different point, and that can be
confusing for dyslexic people and can

00:14:04.443 --> 00:14:06.084
be overwhelming for autistic people.

00:14:06.735 --> 00:14:11.148
So at the end, they decide that they
are going to use the left alignment for

00:14:11.149 --> 00:14:15.902
languages that are reading from left to
right, such as English, and the right

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:21.107
alignment for languages that are reading
from right to left, such as Arabic.

00:14:21.968 --> 00:14:26.339
So now we have changed the
line height and the alignment.

00:14:26.497 --> 00:14:28.158
This looks better and better.

00:14:28.322 --> 00:14:31.802
However, Manuela still
has some suggestions.

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:33.564
What are those suggestions?

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:37.091
First, she wants to talk
about the wall of texts.

00:14:37.866 --> 00:14:42.483
Right now, on this website, we can
see that there are many wall of texts.

00:14:43.039 --> 00:14:48.004
Many wall of texts can be overwhelming for
autistic people, are going to be a torture

00:14:48.021 --> 00:14:52.519
for people with ADHD because they really
have a hard time when processing many

00:14:52.519 --> 00:14:56.776
information at the same time beh- because
they have problems when concentrating,

00:14:57.151 --> 00:15:00.771
and they have to keep the concentration
to read all this information.

00:15:01.428 --> 00:15:07.113
Besides, giving too much information at
the same time can cause anxiety for people

00:15:07.151 --> 00:15:09.572
with anxiety, so this is a bad idea.

00:15:09.717 --> 00:15:17.233
It's better to use small sentences to make
these texts less overwhelming and to use

00:15:17.237 --> 00:15:21.947
bullets of text and bold words because
that way we are going to guide the reading

00:15:21.948 --> 00:15:26.470
for people with ADHD, and we are going
to help them to maintain their attention.

00:15:26.673 --> 00:15:30.903
Then Manuela wants to talk about
the language and the grammatical

00:15:30.988 --> 00:15:32.449
structures they are using.

00:15:32.929 --> 00:15:39.246
Right now, real- they are using
complex language and passive voice and

00:15:39.271 --> 00:15:45.117
sentences like this: "To purchase online
tickets, the timeframe and arrival

00:15:45.124 --> 00:15:49.635
location must be selected, and then
the query trigger must be activated."

00:15:49.868 --> 00:15:52.140
This sentence looks pretty hard.

00:15:52.169 --> 00:15:53.986
It's, it's really complex.

00:15:54.009 --> 00:15:55.363
It has passive voice.

00:15:55.371 --> 00:15:58.990
Passive voice can be, uh,
confusing for autistic people.

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:03.685
We are using weird words, and it's
going to increase the cognitive load.

00:16:03.702 --> 00:16:07.001
So it's better that we use a
language that is, that is so much

00:16:07.003 --> 00:16:11.159
simpler and that is, that we keep
the sentences as short as possible.

00:16:11.501 --> 00:16:16.733
For example, "To purchase flights, choose
dates, destination, and click on Search."

00:16:17.666 --> 00:16:23.583
So we have made those changes again,
and this looks like so much better.

00:16:23.781 --> 00:16:29.478
However, we have changed the, the colors,
the typographies, and even the language.

00:16:29.516 --> 00:16:34.389
But now Manuela wants to change the
whole design of this website because

00:16:34.405 --> 00:16:37.011
the design itself is not accessible.

00:16:37.786 --> 00:16:42.170
Firstly, because we can see at
the right side a video on auto

00:16:42.199 --> 00:16:44.370
video, on, an autoplay loop.

00:16:45.368 --> 00:16:48.973
This video is going to be always
moving, and it's going to be

00:16:48.974 --> 00:16:51.145
distracting for people with ADHD.

00:16:51.882 --> 00:16:56.453
Besides, this website is already
overwhelming because we are asking, we

00:16:56.454 --> 00:17:00.582
are giving the user too much information,
and we are asking the user to select

00:17:00.653 --> 00:17:04.608
the origin and the destination of
their flights, the dates, and so on.

00:17:05.141 --> 00:17:09.116
So we have to keep the design
as clear as possible, and this

00:17:09.127 --> 00:17:12.591
video that is always moving can be
distracting and can be overwhelming.

00:17:13.565 --> 00:17:18.212
On the other hand, we can see that
there are many ads here, ads about

00:17:18.284 --> 00:17:23.411
flights to London, to Boston, to
Paris, and as we have said before,

00:17:23.491 --> 00:17:25.534
this screen is already overwhelming.

00:17:25.588 --> 00:17:28.841
If, we have to avoid using these
ads because they are going to

00:17:28.849 --> 00:17:30.462
be distracting and overwhelming.

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:34.183
Then we can also see that
there are six inputs here.

00:17:34.818 --> 00:17:40.782
The purpose of those inputs is to
let users select their origin and the

00:17:40.783 --> 00:17:42.844
destination of their flights, and so on.

00:17:43.476 --> 00:17:48.365
However, these inputs are really small
and are really too close to each other.

00:17:48.793 --> 00:17:52.249
So people with dyspraxia or with
Tourette syndrome might have a hard

00:17:52.282 --> 00:17:55.988
time because they might click on
the incorrect input because they

00:17:55.988 --> 00:17:57.447
are really too close to each other.

00:17:57.577 --> 00:18:00.755
And lastly, we can see that
there are three buttons here.

00:18:01.513 --> 00:18:05.418
A button to search a flight, a button
to search a flight and a hotel, and a

00:18:05.418 --> 00:18:07.200
button to search a flight and a bike.

00:18:07.723 --> 00:18:09.995
We are giving too many options.

00:18:10.557 --> 00:18:15.688
Autistic people are going to be
overstimulated for these three options.

00:18:16.208 --> 00:18:19.409
People with anxiety are going to
be stressed because they might have

00:18:19.409 --> 00:18:22.254
a hard time when trying to decide
what they are going to choose.

00:18:22.815 --> 00:18:27.804
And people with ADHD already have a hard
time when s- when trying to decide what

00:18:27.804 --> 00:18:32.181
they have to choose, because to decide
they need to maintain their attention, so

00:18:32.783 --> 00:18:35.194
we have to avoid giving so many options.

00:18:35.322 --> 00:18:39.425
So bearing in mind all we have
said, we are going to change the

00:18:39.428 --> 00:18:41.239
whole design of this website.

00:18:41.635 --> 00:18:45.677
And we have changed the design, and
we have made it simpler and clearer.

00:18:46.229 --> 00:18:48.447
We have made the inputs so much bigger.

00:18:48.547 --> 00:18:51.100
We have added spaces between each input.

00:18:51.664 --> 00:18:53.533
We have used bullet points.

00:18:54.081 --> 00:18:58.638
We, uh, small sentences, and we
have left one just single button,

00:18:58.741 --> 00:19:00.118
the button to search a flight.

00:19:00.521 --> 00:19:04.629
So now Manuela fills out every
input, chooses the destination,

00:19:04.638 --> 00:19:06.246
the origin, the dates, and so on.

00:19:06.296 --> 00:19:10.824
And when she clicks on Search
Flight, she's redirected to a list

00:19:10.859 --> 00:19:12.696
of possible flights she can buy.

00:19:13.534 --> 00:19:19.103
However, when she has just arrived to this
website, suddenly a modal appears, a modal

00:19:19.126 --> 00:19:21.932
that says, "Prices will be going up soon.

00:19:22.077 --> 00:19:28.538
Hurry!" And I know this is a marketing
practice, but these models when they

00:19:28.539 --> 00:19:33.224
appear in is a bad idea because it's going
to be overwhelming for autistic people.

00:19:33.511 --> 00:19:37.007
Can cause anxiety for people with
anxiety because they are going to

00:19:37.021 --> 00:19:40.430
think that there are not going to
be enough tickets for everyone.

00:19:40.601 --> 00:19:44.438
And people with ADHD, as we have
said before, they have problems

00:19:44.438 --> 00:19:45.800
controlling their impulsives.

00:19:46.195 --> 00:19:50.706
So if we give them the impression that
there are going to be not enough tickets,

00:19:50.709 --> 00:19:52.685
they are going to do something by impulse.

00:19:52.719 --> 00:19:57.309
So I know this is a marketing thing,
but we have to build products that

00:19:57.309 --> 00:20:01.031
are used in a responsible way,
avoiding these kind of practices.

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:05.973
Then we can see that on each ticket
there is a tick- there is an icon

00:20:05.976 --> 00:20:08.020
of a snail or an icon of a horse.

00:20:08.699 --> 00:20:14.194
The purpose of these icons is to highlight
which one is the fastest ticket and which

00:20:14.197 --> 00:20:16.075
one is the f- is the slowest flight.

00:20:16.133 --> 00:20:21.429
However, these icons can be confusing
for autistic people because ambiguity

00:20:21.449 --> 00:20:24.878
is not something that autistic
people process in a good way.

00:20:25.108 --> 00:20:28.207
So we should erase things
that can be ambiguous.

00:20:28.691 --> 00:20:32.099
Then on each ticket we can
see how many tickets are left.

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:37.925
Again, I know this is a marketing thing,
and it has the objective to make you

00:20:37.945 --> 00:20:41.398
think that you are going to run out of
tickets, so that way you are going to

00:20:41.409 --> 00:20:43.697
buy the tickets as fast as possible.

00:20:44.233 --> 00:20:48.889
But choosing a flight is already
a really complex process.

00:20:48.949 --> 00:20:50.202
It's really overwhelming.

00:20:50.581 --> 00:20:54.117
So we should erase all the
things that are not necessary.

00:20:54.221 --> 00:20:56.617
We don't need to know how
many tickets are left.

00:20:56.837 --> 00:20:59.974
That way we are going to l-
lower the cognitive load.

00:21:00.086 --> 00:21:04.503
Besides, as we have said before, this
kind of practice are not going to help

00:21:04.518 --> 00:21:10.450
people with ADHD because they might
make an, uh, a decision by impulse.

00:21:11.294 --> 00:21:13.617
And now I want to talk about the price.

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:17.931
The price itself is inaccessible
because it's really small.

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:22.505
Hierarchy, it's important when we are
talking about neurodivergent people

00:21:22.553 --> 00:21:28.358
because neurodi- um, because hierarchy
is going to help people understand

00:21:28.375 --> 00:21:30.356
what is the importance of each element.

00:21:30.844 --> 00:21:36.030
The price is one of the most important
things when you have to decide the flight

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:38.749
or when you have to decide for a product.

00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:41.306
So this price should be so much bigger.

00:21:41.412 --> 00:21:47.827
I know, again, this is a practice, uh, a
marketing thing, because marketing doesn't

00:21:47.835 --> 00:21:51.625
like that when something is expensive,
that when a price is too expensive,

00:21:51.648 --> 00:21:54.454
that is shown in a really big way.

00:21:54.656 --> 00:21:56.810
But this price should be so much bigger.

00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:01.816
Besides, the price itself is not
accessible because it's two hundred

00:22:01.818 --> 00:22:04.018
and forty-nine and ninety-nine cents.

00:22:04.848 --> 00:22:05.908
It's too complex.

00:22:06.048 --> 00:22:08.978
People with dyscalculia are
going to have a hard time when

00:22:08.985 --> 00:22:10.541
processing the price itself.

00:22:11.201 --> 00:22:15.591
It's better that we keep it as two hundred
and forty-nine or two hundred and fifty.

00:22:16.129 --> 00:22:21.515
And I know, again, this is a marketing
thing because two hundred forty-nine

00:22:21.604 --> 00:22:26.050
and ninety-nine cents psychologically
is not two hundred and fifty, but in

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:27.767
reality it's two hundred and fifty.

00:22:28.668 --> 00:22:31.651
So it's better that we keep
the price as two hundred and

00:22:31.651 --> 00:22:33.688
forty-nine or two hundred and fifty.

00:22:34.178 --> 00:22:38.002
And if we have to lose ninety-nine
cents during this process, it's

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:41.233
worth it because accessibility
is more important than marketing.

00:22:41.392 --> 00:22:44.918
And lastly, we can see there
is, that there is an asterisk

00:22:44.993 --> 00:22:46.366
at the end of this price.

00:22:46.425 --> 00:22:49.745
People with anxiety are going
to think that something is going

00:22:49.745 --> 00:22:53.129
to be charged later, and they
are going to be really stressed.

00:22:53.241 --> 00:22:56.985
People with, with ADHD are
going to try to find out what

00:22:56.986 --> 00:22:58.340
is the meaning of this asterisk.

00:22:58.724 --> 00:23:02.186
And autistic people can be confused
because they are not going to

00:23:02.187 --> 00:23:06.014
understand what is the meaning or
what is the purpose of this asterisk.

00:23:06.266 --> 00:23:12.097
So we are going to redesign this,
this whole list of, of tickets, and we

00:23:12.099 --> 00:23:16.834
have added at the top of the tickets,
at the top of the list, a way to go

00:23:16.853 --> 00:23:21.709
back and a way to see what we have
selected, what are the origin and the

00:23:21.717 --> 00:23:23.663
destination or of flight and so on.

00:23:23.809 --> 00:23:27.430
This is really important because
people with anxiety likes to

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:30.014
verify everything at all time.

00:23:30.046 --> 00:23:35.710
So giving them the opportunity to know at
all da- at all time what they have, what

00:23:35.710 --> 00:23:38.242
they have chosen is going to calm them.

00:23:38.939 --> 00:23:43.585
Besides, people with ADHD tend to
forget what they have chosen, so

00:23:43.587 --> 00:23:47.495
it's a good idea to always give them
the opportunity to verify what they

00:23:47.496 --> 00:23:49.274
have chosen or what they are doing.

00:23:50.274 --> 00:23:54.889
Next, we have erased the icons of
the horse and the icon of a snail,

00:23:54.969 --> 00:23:58.436
and we have placed it instead the
text the fastest and the slowest.

00:23:59.068 --> 00:24:01.368
That way we are going to be more explicit.

00:24:01.516 --> 00:24:07.164
We have simplified the price itself to
$150, and we have made it so much bigger.

00:24:07.198 --> 00:24:09.703
That way it's going to
be easier to process.

00:24:10.029 --> 00:24:13.260
And we have deleted the asterisk,
and we have added a really

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:15.740
explicit text, plus taxes.

00:24:16.415 --> 00:24:21.504
So now Manuela chooses the first flight
because the second flight is a 16 hours

00:24:21.603 --> 00:24:23.937
long flight, and it's a too long flight.

00:24:24.556 --> 00:24:29.318
So she chooses the first flight,
and then she has to log in on

00:24:29.331 --> 00:24:32.474
this process to keep purchasing.

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:37.550
Nevertheless, I'm so sorry, this
screen is also inaccessible.

00:24:37.908 --> 00:24:38.305
Why?

00:24:38.832 --> 00:24:42.763
Firstly, because when Manuela
is writing her own password, she

00:24:42.764 --> 00:24:44.218
can't see what she's writing.

00:24:44.826 --> 00:24:50.848
I know this is for, is for cybersecurity
reasons, but people with ADHD tend

00:24:50.848 --> 00:24:54.686
to forget what they are doing, so
we have to give them the opportunity

00:24:54.721 --> 00:24:56.339
to check what they have written.

00:24:56.827 --> 00:25:00.776
And people with anxiety also
likes to verify, as we have said

00:25:00.783 --> 00:25:02.670
before, everything at all times.

00:25:03.407 --> 00:25:06.776
On the other hand, people with
dyspraxia or with Tourette syndrome

00:25:06.788 --> 00:25:09.869
might have made a mistake, so
we always have to give them the

00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:12.053
opportunity to see what they are doing.

00:25:12.908 --> 00:25:16.688
Next, as a CAPTCHA, we have
a mathematical operation.

00:25:17.248 --> 00:25:19.710
A really complex mathematical operation.

00:25:20.156 --> 00:25:24.562
Indeed, every group of numbers are
really too close to each other, so this

00:25:24.562 --> 00:25:29.974
is going to be really hard to process
for people with ca- dyscalculia And

00:25:29.977 --> 00:25:36.113
lastly, what if Manuela has forgotten
if she has an account or if she, what

00:25:36.129 --> 00:25:38.322
if she has forgotten her password?

00:25:39.516 --> 00:25:43.858
We have to always give them the
opportunity to recover their account or

00:25:43.858 --> 00:25:49.379
their password because people with ADHD
tend to forget if they, their passwords

00:25:49.431 --> 00:25:54.701
or if they have an account, or people
with anxiety or depression or autistic

00:25:54.703 --> 00:25:58.059
people, when they are overwhelmed,
might forget this kind of information.

00:25:59.035 --> 00:26:03.005
So we're going to change the
whole design of this website.

00:26:03.614 --> 00:26:08.113
We are going to add, again, a way to
go back and a way to see what they

00:26:08.114 --> 00:26:12.797
have selected because keeping the
consistency is important because it's

00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:16.931
going to lower the cognitive load,
and it's going to help neurodivergent

00:26:16.964 --> 00:26:18.532
people process what is happening.

00:26:19.247 --> 00:26:20.745
And we have added an step.

00:26:21.516 --> 00:26:23.450
This stepper is really useful.

00:26:23.489 --> 00:26:28.743
It's useful mostly for, especially
for, uh, people with anxiety because we

00:26:28.743 --> 00:26:32.428
are going to give them the information
about how long it's going to take, the

00:26:32.429 --> 00:26:35.295
whole process, and where are we exactly.

00:26:35.636 --> 00:26:38.105
Giving this kind of
information is really helpful.

00:26:39.097 --> 00:26:43.960
Then we can't see what we are writing
when we are writing the password, but we

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:49.303
have added a button that let us switch
off and switch on the password, so that

00:26:49.321 --> 00:26:54.120
way we are going to be able to verify if
we have written the password correctly.

00:26:55.032 --> 00:27:00.422
Then we have simplified the mathematical
operation, and we have added the spaces

00:27:00.425 --> 00:27:04.869
between each group of number, so that
way it's going to be easier to process.

00:27:05.726 --> 00:27:11.247
However, people with dyscalculia might
still find some difficulties when

00:27:11.255 --> 00:27:16.514
trying to fill out and to solve this
mathematical operation, so we are going

00:27:16.516 --> 00:27:20.899
to add another option, an option to do
the caption with audio verification.

00:27:21.871 --> 00:27:27.353
And to do this, we have added a really
explicit text, text that says, "Audio

00:27:27.353 --> 00:27:33.235
verification by clicking on the icon."
This is important because we, as we have

00:27:33.254 --> 00:27:37.414
been really explicit, autistic people are
going to understand what is the purpose

00:27:37.458 --> 00:27:39.989
of this icon that can be ambiguous.

00:27:40.683 --> 00:27:45.572
Besides, adding this icon is also
useful because people with dyscal- uh,

00:27:45.608 --> 00:27:52.908
dyslexia process in a better way images,
icons, or pictures rather than text.

00:27:52.992 --> 00:27:57.757
So that way we are helping both people
with dyslexia and autistic people.

00:27:58.567 --> 00:28:02.794
And lastly, we have added a way to recover
the password and to recover their account.

00:28:03.699 --> 00:28:09.573
So Manuela logs in, fills out the
mathematical operation, and now she's

00:28:09.604 --> 00:28:12.630
on the most long part of this process.

00:28:13.722 --> 00:28:18.693
Now she has to fill out a really long
form when she has to give her information,

00:28:18.712 --> 00:28:24.603
her data about her passport, her
address, identification, everything.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:31.704
And this screen, this step is,
is, is pretty inaccessible too.

00:28:32.176 --> 00:28:35.900
Firstly, because we can
see as a countdown timer.

00:28:36.481 --> 00:28:39.260
Countdown timer are a really bad idea.

00:28:39.987 --> 00:28:44.497
People with ADHD are going to be
distracted because they are going to see

00:28:44.498 --> 00:28:46.606
something that is moving at all times.

00:28:47.044 --> 00:28:51.139
People with anxiety are going to be
really stressed because they are going

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:55.982
to think that they are not going to have
enough time to fill out every input.

00:28:56.898 --> 00:29:01.485
And people with dyspraxia or with
Tourette syndrome might need more time

00:29:01.488 --> 00:29:03.667
to fill out every element of this form.

00:29:04.088 --> 00:29:08.103
Besides, autistic people
might feel overwhelmed by this

00:29:08.177 --> 00:29:09.838
element moving at all times.

00:29:10.832 --> 00:29:15.362
Next, uh, we have said before, this form
is really long, it's really complex.

00:29:15.396 --> 00:29:17.113
It's already pretty overwhelming.

00:29:17.957 --> 00:29:21.980
When we have an state that is
overwhelming, we should divide it in

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:25.715
different sections or in different
steps because that way we are

00:29:25.718 --> 00:29:27.563
going to lower the cognitive load.

00:29:27.782 --> 00:29:32.767
On the other hand, as we have said
before, this step, this screen is

00:29:32.774 --> 00:29:37.333
already overwhelming and we have
added a decoration, a background

00:29:37.411 --> 00:29:41.713
image that is the, the, the map of
the world and, and it's taking the

00:29:41.719 --> 00:29:43.214
whole height and the whole width.

00:29:44.177 --> 00:29:48.207
As we have said before, this screen is
already overwhelming, so we should keep

00:29:48.222 --> 00:29:53.367
the design as clear as possible, as
simple as possible, because that way we

00:29:53.368 --> 00:29:57.526
are going to lower the cognitive load
and it's going to be easier to process.

00:29:57.776 --> 00:30:02.530
And then to go to the next step, we can
see a button that has the text Next.

00:30:03.348 --> 00:30:07.867
Autistic people are going to be confused
because what does this next mean?

00:30:07.913 --> 00:30:09.619
Where are we going to be lead to?

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:15.669
And people with anxiety that wants to know
everything at all times are going to be

00:30:15.705 --> 00:30:19.590
stressed because they don't know what is
going to happen when they click on Next.

00:30:20.692 --> 00:30:25.402
So we are going to change the design
of this website, again, of this step.

00:30:25.563 --> 00:30:30.965
We are going to add again a way to go back
and a way to see what we have selected

00:30:31.068 --> 00:30:33.391
and the stepper to keep the consistency.

00:30:34.617 --> 00:30:36.260
We have simplified the design.

00:30:37.016 --> 00:30:41.544
We have divided the form in different
steps, so that going-- so that way

00:30:41.554 --> 00:30:43.157
it's going to be easier to process.

00:30:43.669 --> 00:30:48.113
And we have changed the text that
lies on the bottom to buy ticket, so

00:30:48.115 --> 00:30:49.695
that way it's going to be explicit.

00:30:50.510 --> 00:30:55.440
So Manuela fills out every input of this
form, and then clicks on Buy Ticket.

00:30:56.612 --> 00:31:01.369
And then she's on the summary step,
on the last step of this process.

00:31:01.975 --> 00:31:06.156
And this summary step is really important,
because we are giving the information

00:31:06.166 --> 00:31:08.049
about what they are going to buy.

00:31:09.001 --> 00:31:13.200
This is really important information
because people with ADHD, as

00:31:13.213 --> 00:31:16.489
we have said before, tend to
forget what they have chosen.

00:31:16.912 --> 00:31:21.951
So giving them the opportunity to verify
the information is, is highly relevant.

00:31:22.514 --> 00:31:28.211
Besides, people with anxiety, as we have
said before, like to verify everything.

00:31:28.252 --> 00:31:32.060
But it's especially important
when they are going to do

00:31:32.064 --> 00:31:34.061
something that cannot be undone.

00:31:34.131 --> 00:31:37.168
For example, buying some
tickets or buying a product.

00:31:37.673 --> 00:31:42.093
So we have given Manuela the
opportunity to verify everything.

00:31:42.426 --> 00:31:46.195
She has checked that the arrival
and the departure are correct,

00:31:46.341 --> 00:31:47.959
and she clicks on Buy Ticket.

00:31:48.235 --> 00:31:53.199
And now she, she's on the step
when she can see that she has buy--

00:31:53.213 --> 00:31:54.708
bought those tickets correctly.

00:31:55.155 --> 00:31:58.719
We are giving again the information
about what she has bought,

00:31:59.453 --> 00:32:01.294
and we have added two buttons.

00:32:01.785 --> 00:32:03.327
A button to see the tickets.

00:32:03.528 --> 00:32:06.899
That is going to be useful for
people with ADHD and people with

00:32:06.901 --> 00:32:08.553
anxiety, as we have said before.

00:32:09.339 --> 00:32:11.106
And a button to search an hotel.

00:32:11.309 --> 00:32:14.556
That is going to guide this
user during the process of

00:32:14.558 --> 00:32:16.594
buying or, or finding a hotel.

00:32:17.199 --> 00:32:22.754
These two buttons are important because
if we don't give options to the, to the

00:32:22.761 --> 00:32:26.595
people that reach this step, they are
going to be confused or they are going

00:32:26.595 --> 00:32:31.296
to be trapped, and the situation can
be overwhelming and can be stressful.

00:32:32.223 --> 00:32:37.589
So now you might be wondering if Manuela
and An- and Ana has been able to meet

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:39.304
each other for the very first time.

00:32:39.484 --> 00:32:45.771
But Ana, during this process, has
realized something Not only is

00:32:45.772 --> 00:32:51.785
important to design taking into account
neurodivergences, it's also important

00:32:51.866 --> 00:32:56.523
to write good code to improve the
accessibility for neurodivergent people.

00:32:57.538 --> 00:33:02.508
So what do we have to bear in mind
when we are talking about code and

00:33:02.875 --> 00:33:04.987
accessibility for neurodivergent people?

00:33:05.252 --> 00:33:10.463
Firstly, we should use semantic
HTML and ARIA attributes, as both

00:33:10.464 --> 00:33:14.081
of them adds important information
to an assistive technologies.

00:33:14.236 --> 00:33:18.725
Neurodivergent people might use
assistive technologies, for example,

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:23.101
because they process in a better way
with the ears than with the eyes.

00:33:23.132 --> 00:33:27.986
So it's important that we use both
semantic HTML and ARIA attributes.

00:33:28.489 --> 00:33:32.419
Also, it's important that we
keep the focus always visible.

00:33:32.966 --> 00:33:37.106
People with dyspraxia or with Tourette
syndrome might have a hard time when

00:33:37.106 --> 00:33:43.068
using a mouse, so they rather use the
keyboard navigation instead of a mouse.

00:33:43.139 --> 00:33:48.676
So it's important that we keep this
focus always visible, because otherwise

00:33:48.715 --> 00:33:53.316
they can be confused, as they are not
going to be able to know where they are.

00:33:53.553 --> 00:33:59.270
In the same way, we have to manage the
focus in a way that makes sense, that they

00:33:59.270 --> 00:34:05.308
see the element receiving the focus on the
same order they can be seen in the design.

00:34:05.964 --> 00:34:10.603
Also, that when they open a modal, the
focus moves to the modal, and so on.

00:34:10.619 --> 00:34:14.110
That we manage a focus in general
in a way that makes sense.

00:34:14.677 --> 00:34:21.081
Then, uh, some people on some devices can
tell those devices that they want to avoid

00:34:21.188 --> 00:34:23.525
movements, animation, or transitions.

00:34:24.391 --> 00:34:28.337
This is relevant because movements,
animation, and transitions can be

00:34:28.339 --> 00:34:32.371
distracting for people with ADHD
and can be overwhelming for autistic

00:34:32.375 --> 00:34:35.085
people or for people with anxiety.

00:34:35.315 --> 00:34:41.375
So on our CSS, we can tell our
CSS to avoid this kind of movement

00:34:41.411 --> 00:34:45.241
and animations, and we have to
take into account the situation.

00:34:45.882 --> 00:34:51.221
On the other hand, it's important that we
have a dark mode and a light mode, because

00:34:51.301 --> 00:34:56.103
autistic people might feel overwhelmed
by a dark mode or by a light mode.

00:34:56.163 --> 00:34:57.450
It depends on the person.

00:34:57.476 --> 00:35:02.258
So having both of them are going to help
them choose what fits better for them.

00:35:03.258 --> 00:35:08.523
Similarly, allowing customization is
going to be useful for dyslexic people.

00:35:09.048 --> 00:35:12.966
As we have said before, absolute white
and absolute black, it depends on the

00:35:12.966 --> 00:35:18.139
dyslexic person, of course, uh, can be
difficult to process for dyslexic people.

00:35:18.156 --> 00:35:22.688
So allowing them to customize
the, the website or the

00:35:22.688 --> 00:35:24.645
platform is going to help them.

00:35:25.228 --> 00:35:30.035
Sometimes, not always, we can
decide what happens by default.

00:35:30.785 --> 00:35:34.970
When we have the opportunity to decide
what happens by default, we have to

00:35:34.971 --> 00:35:37.146
choose the less overwhelming option.

00:35:37.899 --> 00:35:43.023
For example, if we have a carousel, it's
better that the carousel stays static.

00:35:43.641 --> 00:35:47.982
That is not changing from one sl-
from one slide to the next one

00:35:48.089 --> 00:35:53.121
automatically, because that movement
can be distracting for people with ADHD

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:57.503
and can be overwhelming for autistic
people or for people with anxiety.

00:35:57.799 --> 00:35:59.673
Another example is the dropdowns.

00:36:00.110 --> 00:36:02.318
Let's imagine that we have many dropdowns.

00:36:03.018 --> 00:36:08.301
It's better to keep all these dropdowns
closed, because if all of them are

00:36:08.336 --> 00:36:13.460
open, we might be displaying too much
information at the same time, and that can

00:36:13.526 --> 00:36:18.685
cause anxiety, can be overwhelming, and
can be distracting for people with ADHD.

00:36:19.141 --> 00:36:21.988
On the other hand, I would
like to talk about the scrolls.

00:36:23.072 --> 00:36:27.634
Sometimes when we click on a link,
we are redirected to a different

00:36:27.635 --> 00:36:29.681
part of a, of the same page.

00:36:30.235 --> 00:36:35.374
To make this movement more aesthetic,
we use the behavior smooth to make this

00:36:35.375 --> 00:36:41.807
movement, to make this, um, to make the
user slide smoothly to the next part.

00:36:42.518 --> 00:36:48.940
However, this smooth slide can be
distracting for people with ADHD and

00:36:49.314 --> 00:36:51.194
can be distressful for autistic people.

00:36:51.458 --> 00:36:56.068
So it's better that we keep the
behavior of this scroll by default.

00:36:56.430 --> 00:36:59.448
We should also avoid automatic timeouts.

00:37:00.601 --> 00:37:04.167
Because they can be, uh,
overwhelming for autistic people.

00:37:04.337 --> 00:37:07.498
Autistic people are going to think
that they have made something

00:37:07.499 --> 00:37:08.877
to provoke the situation.

00:37:09.285 --> 00:37:13.200
People with ADHD are going to think
that they have made something, that they

00:37:13.203 --> 00:37:15.790
have forgotten to provoke the situation.

00:37:16.420 --> 00:37:21.303
And people with anxiety are going to think
that they are, have made a mistake and

00:37:21.304 --> 00:37:23.795
they have provoked the whole situation.

00:37:24.252 --> 00:37:28.095
So it's better that we ke- give
them the information beforehand

00:37:28.169 --> 00:37:29.635
about what is going to happen.

00:37:30.403 --> 00:37:36.013
For example, if we have to log out this
user because his, his or her session

00:37:36.027 --> 00:37:40.588
has expired, it's better that we inform
that this situation is going to happen.

00:37:40.635 --> 00:37:46.605
Because that way when suddenly they
are… Well, suddenly, they are,

00:37:46.623 --> 00:37:50.207
they, they are logged out, they are
going to understand what happened, and

00:37:50.208 --> 00:37:51.940
they are not going to be distressed.

00:37:52.244 --> 00:37:55.615
It's also important that we
create user-friendly forms.

00:37:56.092 --> 00:38:01.148
Let's imagine for a second that we
are on, on the stepper that, where

00:38:01.158 --> 00:38:05.366
Manuela had to put all her information
on the form, and we are filling out

00:38:05.410 --> 00:38:07.318
each input of a really long form.

00:38:07.460 --> 00:38:11.287
And when we clicked on the submit
button, suddenly many errors appear.

00:38:11.582 --> 00:38:13.312
Many errors are shown.

00:38:13.629 --> 00:38:16.997
This situation is going to be
a torture for people with ADHD.

00:38:17.722 --> 00:38:22.078
People with ADHD already have a
hard time with filling out each

00:38:22.102 --> 00:38:26.538
input on this form, and now they
have to go back to fix every input.

00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:31.762
So many errors showing up at the
same time is going to be overwhelming

00:38:31.765 --> 00:38:35.978
for autistic people and can cause
anxiety for people with anxiety.

00:38:36.612 --> 00:38:41.191
Also, uh, people with dyspraxia or
with Tourette syndrome already have

00:38:41.192 --> 00:38:45.756
a hard time with filling out this
really long form, and now they have

00:38:45.756 --> 00:38:47.552
to go back to verify everything.

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:53.556
So it's better that we give them the
opportunity to know if they have fill

00:38:53.556 --> 00:38:58.781
out every input in a correct way,
because that way they are going to

00:38:58.781 --> 00:39:01.304
be able to fix everything on the fly.

00:39:01.778 --> 00:39:06.851
Lastly, it's important that we build
an accessibility culture on our teams,

00:39:06.931 --> 00:39:11.026
and that we become evangelists of the
accessibility for neurodivergent people.

00:39:11.423 --> 00:39:14.000
So what are the conclusions of this talk?

00:39:14.300 --> 00:39:19.686
Firstly, we have to create designs that
are simply and are clear, because that way

00:39:19.710 --> 00:39:22.039
we are going to lower the cognitive load.

00:39:23.020 --> 00:39:27.127
Using an structure that makes sense
and hierarchy are going to help

00:39:27.149 --> 00:39:30.738
neurodivergent people, because they
are going to be able to process

00:39:30.783 --> 00:39:32.701
in a easier way what is happening.

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:38.183
We have to divide the parts of the
process or the parts of the screens

00:39:38.242 --> 00:39:42.523
that are really complex into different
sections or into different steps.

00:39:43.163 --> 00:39:46.944
And keeping the consistency is
going to help neurodivergent people

00:39:47.022 --> 00:39:48.805
understand what is happening.

00:39:48.980 --> 00:39:55.191
Then, accessibility should never be at
the service of marketing, as accessibility

00:39:55.229 --> 00:39:56.945
is more important than marketing.

00:39:57.345 --> 00:40:01.312
And I would like to thank, uh, WordPress
